Scope check: `git status --porcelain` diff confined to the three manifest files
(.swarm/manifests/T18.2.files: internal/handlers/whatif/handlers_income_expense.go,
internal/handlers/whatif/onetime_expense_test.go,
internal/services/retirement/analysis/one_time_expense_test.go). No out-of-scope
edits. `go build ./...` clean, `go vet ./...` clean, full `go test ./...` — all
packages `ok`, no FAILs. `.swarm/tier3/T18/accept.sh` → ORACLE PASS.

Attacks that FAILED to break the attempt-2 delta (verified, not eyeballed):

1. Clone reality. `retirementMgr.Load()` returns `prepare.Clone(sm.cache)` (a
   real deep copy per settings.go:427-471 doc comment and code), so
   `current.OneTimeExpenses = append(...)` in the handler's pre-check does not
   alias the live cache. Confirmed by reading Load/LoadContext.

2. 4xx path honesty / no state pollution. Probe test (POST year=30 → 4xx,
   then POST a valid entry → 200, then reload): the valid "Good" entry
   persists, the rejected "Bad" entry never appears in the reloaded settings.
   PASS.

3. Backtest test not vacuous. `meanFinalBalance` calls `t.Fatal` on an empty
   results slice, so a mean-of-empty-NaN escape is not possible. Mutation-
   tested by hand: backed up stepper.go (sha256), replaced
   `oneTimeExpenseThisMonth = OneTimeExpensesForYear(s, currentYear)` with
   `= 0.0`, re-ran `TestOneTimeExpense_HistoricalBacktestPicksItUp` — it FAILS
   ("diff=0.00 ... may have been dropped from the backtest loop"), confirming
   real signal. Restored file, `sha256sum -c` confirmed byte-identical
   restore.

4. `TestOneTimeExpense_HandlerAdd_YearBeyondHorizonRejected` genuinely
   reloads settings and asserts non-persistence (not just status code), and
   asserts a follow-up GET /whatif is 200 — this is the correct regression
   test for attempt 1's specific defect and it passes.

ATTACK THAT SUCCEEDED — same severity class as attempt 1, reproduced live:

5. TOCTOU / alternate write path, exactly the angle the dispatch brief named.
   `AddOneTimeExpense` (settings.go:1646) itself does NOT validate — it only
   appends and saves. The new safety is entirely a property of the ONE
   handler `handleWhatIfAddOneTime`. `UpdateSettings` (settings.go:1109,
   `applySettingsUpdates` line ~1301: `settings.ProjectionYears = v`) writes
   `projection_years` unconditionally, with **no call to
   `ValidateOneTimeExpenses`** anywhere in that path. This is not a
   contrived internal-only entry point: `web/templates/components/whatif/
   portfolio-settings.html:85` has a live `#projection-years-slider` on the
   /whatif page itself (quick-adjust control), which posts straight through
   `UpdateSettings`.

   Reproduced with a probe test (`internal/handlers/whatif/
   zz_t18_shrink_probe_test.go`, added then removed, `git status --porcelain`
   confirmed clean before/after this session):
   - `rm.AddOneTimeExpense({Year: 29, ...})` with default ProjectionYears=30
     — valid, saves fine.
   - Pre-shrink `GET /whatif` → 200 (sanity).
   - `rm.UpdateSettings({"projection_years": 20})` — succeeds, no validation.
   - Post-shrink `GET /whatif` → **500**, body is the same bare
     `renderError` fragment (handlers.go:614-627) attempt 1 produced, not the
     full page — because `handleWhatIf` calls `runAnalysisWithCache` →
     `prepare.From` → `ValidateOneTimeExpenses` unconditionally
     (prepare.go:47-63, added by this same task), and that now rejects the
     year-29 entry against the new horizon of 20.
   - There is still no UI path back to delete the offending entry once the
     page is bricked (the delete button lives on the page that no longer
     renders).

   This is not a preexisting/precedented pattern: `prepare.From` had no
   analogous hard-validate-and-500 gate for any other collection before this
   task (`BigTicketItem`, e.g., is never checked against `ProjectionYears` in
   validate.go/prepare.go at all). The unconditional-500-on-render behavior
   at `prepare.From` is new, introduced by this task's own
   `ValidateOneTimeExpenses` call, and the worker guarded exactly one of the
   (at least) two ordinary, UI-reachable ways to violate the invariant it
   enforces. This matches the dispatch brief's explicit acid test ("if yes,
   that is the SAME severity class as attempt 1's defect ... judge
   accordingly") — a normal action (dragging the projection-years slider
   down after adding a one-time expense near the current horizon) bricks the
   /whatif page with no recovery path through the UI.

Given a reproduced, evidence-backed, user-facing bricking defect of the same
severity class as attempt 1's, gated by a validation seam this very task
introduced, and reachable through a live UI control on the same page, attempt
2 does not meet the criteria's implied safety bar any more completely than
attempt 1 did — it only narrowed the entry point. VERDICT: FAIL.
